    "case_id": "131",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old man presented with a three-week history of a tender, slow-growing left axillary lump and a low-grade fever (37.5–38°C) for one week. His medical history was notable for hypertension—treated with lacidipine, lisinopril, and hydrochlorothiazide—and a laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass performed two years earlier, after which he lost 112 kg (BMI from 64.6 to 28 kg/m2). He smoked 30 cigarettes daily and took vitamin (A, D, E, B complex), calcium, cholecalciferol, and iron supplements. There was no personal or family history of autoimmune or lymphoproliferative disease.\n\nOn examination, he had a tender, non-mobile, 3 × 4 cm lymph node in the left axilla; the remainder of the exam was unremarkable. Laboratory studies—including a complete blood count, ESR, liver and renal panels, autoantibodies (ANA, RF), complement levels, inflammatory markers, thyroid function, and serologies for a broad spectrum of viral and parasitic pathogens—were all normal or negative. A thoracoabdominal CT scan was unremarkable except for the axillary lymphadenopathy. A biopsy of the axillary node revealed coagulative necrosis with frequent karyorrhexis and a lymphoplasmacytoid infiltrate. An investigation for mycobacteria was negative.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Histiocytic necrotizing lymphadenitis\nB. Systemic lupus erythematosus, malignant lymphoma, and pyogenic infection were considered but excluded by lymph node\nC. Tuberculous lymphadenitis was considered but excluded, as “An investigation for mycobacteria was negative\nD. None\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
